WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.505
Begin the threat model with assets.

00:00:03.505 --> 00:00:14.489
These include application workloads, data, secrets, container images, nodes, registries, clusters, build systems, deployment pipelines, and the identities used to operate them.

00:00:14.489 --> 00:00:19.397
Rank assets by confidentiality, integrity, availability, and business impact.

00:00:19.397 --> 00:00:21.967
Next, map trust boundaries.

00:00:21.967 --> 00:00:37.625
Important boundaries exist between the container and host, workload and runtime, node and orchestrator, application and network, identity and service account, workload and storage, registry and deployment, and CI/CD system and cluster.

00:00:37.625 --> 00:00:42.065
Trace data and control flows across those boundaries.

00:00:42.065 --> 00:00:51.646
Record which components can create workloads, select images, mount storage, request secrets, schedule pods, administer nodes, or change policy.

00:00:51.646 --> 00:00:57.488
The model should show both intended reachability and explicitly prohibited reachability.

00:00:57.488 --> 00:01:01.929
Attacker goals can be described without exploitation detail.

00:01:01.929 --> 00:01:11.977
A compromised workload may seek greater host influence, access to secrets, persistence, interference with neighboring workloads, or broader platform control.

00:01:11.977 --> 00:01:17.820
Defenders should state which assumptions are supposed to prevent each outcome.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.858
Build scenarios around control failure.

00:01:20.858 --> 00:01:33.243
Ask what happens if a workload identity is misused, a privileged deployment is approved, a node is compromised, or a registry serves an untrusted image.

00:01:33.243 --> 00:01:39.553
Estimate blast radius based on permissions, tenancy, network access, and recovery dependencies.

00:01:39.553 --> 00:01:42.591
Finish with evidence and ownership.

00:01:42.591 --> 00:01:51.705
Identify admission records, runtime events, Kubernetes audit data, node telemetry, image metadata, and configuration sources that validate assumptions.

00:01:51.705 --> 00:01:59.884
Assign owners to reduce risk, accept exceptions, monitor controls, and revisit the model after architectural change.
